From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PeripheralCecAdapter: follow held volume keys and the amp's
 level

Volume keys reached the bus as a held key whatever the remote did: presses
within 100 ms of each other were dropped, the others went out as further
<User Control Pressed>, and one <User Control Released> followed 250 ms
after the last. Separate presses merged into a hold, a mute key could be
left held long enough for the amp to toggle it again, and the amp's level
was only asked for after the release, so the volume bar stood still while
the keys were pressed.

Tell the adapter whether an action repeats a held key (its hold time).
A single press goes out as a press and a release. A held volume key goes
out as a press repeated every 100 ms while the remote repeats it, and one
release once the repeats stop for 150 ms; the remote repeats every ~33 ms,
faster than the bus can carry a press and a release each, so sending each
repeat as its own press queued up seconds of keys after the remote was
let go.

Ignore the repeats of a held mute key in CApplication, before the
peripherals see them: they toggled mute back and forth, through the amp
and through Kodi's own mixer alike. A long press mapped to mute sends
its first action with a hold time too, so drop only a held mute action
that comes within a second of the last one.

Ask the amp for its audio status from the adapter thread, one request at
a time and the next 50 ms after each answer, until a request sent after
the last key is answered, so the level in the volume bar follows the amp
while the keys go out. A request the amp does not answer within 300 ms is
sent again, for up to 2 s. This replaces the blocking retries the request
made before.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/xbmc/application/Application.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/application/Application.cpp
@@ -1424,6 +1424,18 @@
     }
   }
 
+  /* a held mute key repeats, which would toggle mute back and forth. a long press mapped to mute
+     sends its first action held too, so only a held action that follows another one is dropped */
+  if (action.GetID() == ACTION_MUTE)
+  {
+    static std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point lastMute;
+    const auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
+    const bool bRepeat = action.GetHoldTime() > 0 && now - lastMute < std::chrono::seconds(1);
+    lastMute = now;
+    if (bRepeat)
+      return true;
+  }
+
   if (CServiceBroker::GetPeripherals().OnAction(action))
     return true;
 
